.TH TFIDF 1 "June 2026" "tfidf-rs 0.0.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tfidf \- composable TF-IDF document indexing and similarity search
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tfidf
.B save
[\fB--index\fR \fIPATH\fR]
[\fIPATH\fR...]
[\fB--\fR]
.PP
.B tfidf
.B save
.I INDEX
[\fIPATH\fR...]
[\fB--\fR]
.PP
.B tfidf
.B query
[\fB--index\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.B list
.PP
.B tfidf
.B query
[\fB--index\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.B terms
[\fB--top\fR \fIN\fR]
.PP
.B tfidf
.B query
[\fB--index\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.B similar
.I FILE
[\fB--top\fR \fIN\fR]
.PP
.B tfidf
.B query
[\fB--index\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.B search
[\fIQUERY\fR]
[\fB--top\fR \fIN\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tfidf
builds a serialized TF-IDF index from files and directories, then queries it
using pipe-friendly commands.
.PP
The program is designed for shell composition. Normal query output is plain
tab-delimited text with no decoration, progress display, or implicit stdout
formatting. Broken pipes are handled gracefully, so commands such as
.BR head (1)
and
.BR tail (1)
can terminate pipelines without noisy diagnostics.
.PP
Documents are tokenized into ASCII word tokens, lowercased, optionally filtered
through a stopword file, and represented as sparse TF-IDF vectors. Similarity is
computed using cosine similarity.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS save
.B tfidf save
builds an index from one or more input files or directories and writes it to the
resolved index path.
.PP
If an input path is a directory, it is walked recursively. Regular files found
beneath it are included in the corpus. Files that cannot be read or that produce
no tokens are skipped.
.PP
If
.B --index
is supplied, that path is used for the output index. Otherwise
.B TFIDF_INDEX
or the default data-dir index path is used.
.PP
For compatibility,
.B tfidf save INDEX PATH...
is also accepted. The first positional argument is treated as a legacy index path
when it looks like an index file, has extension
.BR .index ,
has basename
.B index
or
.BR tfidf.index ,
or when it does not exist and at least one more positional argument is present.
.PP
If
.B --
appears, additional input paths are read from standard input, one path per line.
Empty lines are ignored.
.SS query list
Lists all document paths stored in the index.
.PP
Output format:
.PP
.RS
.nf
PATH
.fi
.RE
.SS query terms
Shows the top terms by aggregate TF-IDF score across all indexed documents.
.PP
Output format:
.PP
.RS
.nf
TERM<TAB>SCORE
.fi
.RE
.PP
Scores are printed with six digits after the decimal point.
.SS query similar
Finds documents similar to the indexed document named by
.IR FILE .
.PP
The file argument must exactly match the stored document path in the index.
Use
.B tfidf query list
to inspect indexed paths.
.PP
Output format:
.PP
.RS
.nf
PATH<TAB>SCORE
.fi
.RE
.PP
Only positive similarity scores are emitted.
.SS query search
Searches indexed documents using a query string. The query is tokenized with the
same tokenizer and stopword configuration used for indexing.
.PP
If
.I QUERY
is omitted, or if the first argument is
.BR --top ,
the query text is read from standard input.
.PP
When passing a multi-word query as an argument, quote it in the shell:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tfidf query search "error handling rust"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Output format:
.PP
.RS
.nf
PATH<TAB>SCORE
.fi
.RE
.PP
Only positive similarity scores are emitted.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --index " PATH"
Use
.I PATH
as the index file. This option is recognized by both
.B save
and
.BR query .
It has the highest precedence in index path resolution.
.TP
.BI --top " N"
Limit result count to
.IR N .
Supported by
.BR terms ,
.BR similar ,
and
.BR search .
If omitted or invalid, the command default is used.
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Print usage information.
.SH DEFAULTS
.TP
Default application name
.B tfidf
.TP
Default index file name
.B tfidf.index
.TP
Default number of terms
.B tfidf query terms
prints 20 terms.
.TP
Default number of similar documents
.B tfidf query similar
prints 10 documents.
.TP
Default number of search results
.B tfidf query search
prints 10 documents.
.TP
Default stopword behavior
No stopwords are used unless a stopword file exists in the configured location.
.SH INDEX PATH RESOLUTION
The index path is resolved in this order:
.IP "1." 4
.B --index
.IR PATH .
.IP "2." 4
.B TFIDF_INDEX
environment variable.
.IP "3." 4
The default XDG-style data path:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$(tfidf data dir)/tfidf.index
.fi
.RE
.PP
The TF-IDF data directory is resolved in this order:
.IP "1." 4
.B TFIDF_DATA_DIR
as an explicit directory. When this variable is set, the application name is not
appended.
.IP "2." 4
.BR XDG_DATA_HOME / TFIDF_APPNAME
.IP "3." 4
.BR HOME /.local/share/ TFIDF_APPNAME
.IP "4." 4
.BR ./ TFIDF_APPNAME
if no home directory is available.
.PP
.B TFIDF_APPNAME
defaults to
.BR tfidf .
.SH CONFIGURATION FILES
.SS Stopwords
Stopwords are loaded from the first existing file in this order:
.IP "1." 4
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME / TFIDF_APPNAME /stopwords.txt
.IP "2." 4
.BR HOME /.config/ TFIDF_APPNAME /stopwords.txt
.PP
The stopword file is line-oriented. Each non-empty line is trimmed, lowercased,
and treated as one stopword.
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
the
a
an
of
and
.fi
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TFIDF_INDEX
Default index file path used when
.B --index
is not supplied.
.TP
.B TFIDF_DATA_DIR
Base data directory used to construct the default index path. If set, the
default index path is:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$TFIDF_DATA_DIR/tfidf.index
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B TFIDF_APPNAME
Application namespace for XDG data and config paths. Defaults to
.BR tfidf .
Set this to maintain separate indexes and stopword configurations for different
projects.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Used when
.B TFIDF_DATA_DIR
is unset. The data directory becomes:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$XDG_DATA_HOME/$TFIDF_APPNAME
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Used to locate stopwords:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/$TFIDF_APPNAME/stopwords.txt
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B HOME
Used as a fallback for data and config paths:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$HOME/.local/share/$TFIDF_APPNAME/tfidf.index
$HOME/.config/$TFIDF_APPNAME/stopwords.txt
.fi
.RE
.SH TOKENIZATION
Input bytes are split into word tokens.
.PP
A word byte is:
.IP \(bu 2
ASCII alphabetic,
.IP \(bu 2
ASCII digit, or
.IP \(bu 2
underscore.
.PP
ASCII alphabetic bytes are lowercased. Other bytes delimit tokens.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
Hello, world! test-case
.fi
.RE
.PP
becomes:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hello
world
test
case
.fi
.RE
.PP
The tokenizer is ASCII-oriented. Non-ASCII bytes are token delimiters.
.SH SCORING
Term frequency is computed as:
.PP
.RS
.nf
TF(t,d) = count(t,d) / |d|
.fi
.RE
.PP
Inverse document frequency is computed with smoothing:
.PP
.RS
.nf
IDF(t) = ln((D + 1) / (df(t) + 1))
.fi
.RE
.PP
TF-IDF is:
.PP
.RS
.nf
TFIDF(t,d) = TF(t,d) * IDF(t)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Document similarity is cosine similarity over sparse TF-IDF vectors:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cos(a,b) = (a . b) / (||a|| ||b||)
.fi
.RE
.SH OUTPUT
All normal query output is suitable for common shell tools such as
.BR cut (1),
.BR sort (1),
.BR awk (1),
.BR xargs (1),
and
.BR head (1).
.PP
No headers are printed. Scores use fixed six-decimal formatting.
.PP
Diagnostic messages and usage text are written to standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Build an index using the default resolved index path:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tfidf save src/ notes/
.fi
.RE
.PP
Build an index explicitly:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tfidf save --index docs.index src/ notes/
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use the legacy positional index form:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tfidf save docs.index src/ notes/
.fi
.RE
.PP
Read paths from standard input:
.PP
.RS
.nf
find src notes -type f | tfidf save --index docs.index --
.fi
.RE
.PP
List indexed documents:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tfidf query --index docs.index list
.fi
.RE
.PP
Show the top 100 terms:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tfidf query --index docs.index terms --top 100
.fi
.RE
.PP
Sort terms by score:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tfidf query terms --top 100 | sort -k2,2nr
.fi
.RE
.PP
Find documents similar to an indexed file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tfidf query similar src/main.rs --top 10
.fi
.RE
.PP
Open similar files through a pipeline:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tfidf query similar src/main.rs | cut -f1 | xargs ${PAGER:-less}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Search with a quoted query string:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tfidf query search "error handling rust" --top 20
.fi
.RE
.PP
Search with query text from standard input:
.PP
.RS
.nf
echo "memory management" | tfidf query search --top 10
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use an environment-selected index:
.PP
.RS
.nf
export TFIDF_INDEX=$PWD/docs.index
tfidf save src/ notes/
tfidf query terms
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use a separate application namespace:
.PP
.RS
.nf
export TFIDF_APPNAME=project-docs
mkdir -p ~/.config/project-docs
printf '%s\\n' the and of > ~/.config/project-docs/stopwords.txt
tfidf save ~/work/docs
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.BR $TFIDF_DATA_DIR/tfidf.index
Default index path when
.B TFIDF_DATA_DIR
is set.
.TP
.BR $XDG_DATA_HOME/$TFIDF_APPNAME/tfidf.index
Default index path when
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
is set and
.B TFIDF_DATA_DIR
is unset.
.TP
.BR $HOME/.local/share/$TFIDF_APPNAME/tfidf.index
Fallback default index path.
.TP
.BR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/$TFIDF_APPNAME/stopwords.txt
Primary stopword file.
.TP
.BR $HOME/.config/$TFIDF_APPNAME/stopwords.txt
Fallback stopword file.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Usage error, missing input paths, missing index, empty search query, unknown
command, or another fatal error.
.PP
When stdout is closed by a downstream command in a pipeline,
.B tfidf
exits successfully without printing a broken-pipe error.
.SH NOTES
The index is serialized with
.BR bincode .
The on-disk format is an implementation detail and should not be treated as a
stable interchange format.
.PP
Index creation replaces the target index file. Incremental update operations are
not currently exposed.
.PP
The
.B search
command uses the first non-option query argument as the query string. Quote
multi-word queries so they are passed as one shell argument, or pipe the query on
standard input.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR find (1),
.BR cut (1),
.BR sort (1),
.BR awk (1),
.BR xargs (1),
.BR head (1)
